<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android">

  <uses-feature android:name="android.hardware.type.watch" />

  <!--
    READ THIS BEFORE WIRING UP THE PROMPT.

    This permission is one-shot and unrecoverable. Once the user denies it, it
    CANNOT be re-requested, and setWatchFaceAsActive() may only ever be called
    once regardless. Deny it and that user is permanently stuck setting faces
    by hand via long-press.

    It is the only irreversible action in the whole system. Design where in the
    flow you ask before you write the code that asks. See docs/SPEC.md.
  -->
  <!--
    Required to call the Watch Face Push API AT ALL. Distinct from the
    activation permission below, and easy to miss: the library's own manifest
    declares no permission, only a <queries> entry, so nothing in the build
    tells you this is needed.

    Without it, every Push call fails with

      SecurityException: Not allowed to bind to service Intent {
        act=com.google.wear.ACTION_PUSH_WATCH_FACES ... }

    which names bindService rather than a missing permission. Read off the
    watch itself on 2026-08-29 — `dumpsys package
    com.google.android.wearable.dwf.receiver` shows WatchFaceReceiverService
    guarded by exactly this string. See DECISIONS.md.
  -->
  <!--
    Needed to post the notification that carries the activation ask. Android
    will not let the install path open the dialog directly — the face lands in a
    background context and the launch is blocked — so the notification IS the
    route to the one irreversible question. Without this permission `notify` is
    a silent no-op and the ask simply never happens.
  -->
  <!--
    WITHOUT THIS THE SLOT PICKER CAN ONLY OFFER OUR OWN COMPLICATION.

    From Android 11 a package is invisible to queryIntentServices unless it is
    declared here, and NEITHER launcher activities NOR services for a custom
    action are automatically visible. minSdk is 36, so the filtering is fully
    in force and ProviderCatalog was querying into a room with one thing in it.

    The symptom is confusing because it looks device-specific: a bare EMULATOR
    reported 37 providers, a real Pixel Watch 5 reported exactly one — ours. The
    difference is not the query, it is what counts as automatically visible.
    An emulator's apps are preinstalled system packages, which are; Fitbit and
    anything else that arrives through Play is not. Reported by the operator on
    2026-09-19 as "I don't see any of the Google health options", and the reason
    he could not see them is that the watch app could not either.

    The MAIN/LAUNCHER intent is here for the same reason. `launchable()` appears
    to work today, but only because the apps it happens to find — Play services,
    the Play Store, the media app — are system packages. Anything installed by a
    person was already invisible to it, silently.
  -->
  <queries>
    <intent>
      <action android:name="android.support.wearable.complications.ACTION_COMPLICATION_UPDATE_REQUEST" />
    </intent>
    <intent>
      <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
      <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
    </intent>
  </queries>

  <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.POST_NOTIFICATIONS" />

  <uses-permission android:name="com.google.wear.permission.PUSH_WATCH_FACES" />

  <uses-permission android:name="com.google.wear.permission.SET_PUSHED_WATCH_FACE_AS_ACTIVE" />

  <application
      android:fullBackupContent="@xml/backup_rules"
      android:dataExtractionRules="@xml/data_extraction_rules"
      android:label="BFG Watch Faces"
      android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
      android:theme="@style/Theme.BfgWatchFaces"
      android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round">
    <!--
      TRUE, and this is what stops the app appearing in the Play Store on a
      watch when it is false.

      "Non-standalone" is the Wear 2.x arrangement: the watch app was not
      independently installable and Play pushed it out automatically when the
      phone app was installed. Wear OS 3 removed that delivery, so a
      non-standalone app is simply not offered on a modern watch — which is
      exactly the symptom, an app released to a track that never appears in the
      list.

      It was false because when it was written this app had no launcher activity
      at all: there was nothing to open and nothing to install on its own. That
      stopped being true when WatchActivity landed. The app now installs, opens,
      and tells you whether it is ready and whether it may switch your face. It
      needs the phone app to be USEFUL, which is a different question from
      whether it can stand on its own.
    -->
    <meta-data android:name="com.google.android.wearable.standalone" android:value="true" />

    <!--
      Receives a face from the device over ChannelClient and installs it.
      addWatchFace takes a ParcelFileDescriptor — a LOCAL file — and there is
      no "send to the paired watch" call anywhere in the API, so something on
      the watch has to be holding the bytes. That is the whole reason this app
      exists; see docs/SPEC.md, "Why there are two apps".
    -->
    <service
        android:name=".FaceReceiverService"
        android:exported="true">
      <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="com.google.android.gms.wearable.CHANNEL_EVENT" />
        <data android:scheme="wear" android:host="*" android:pathPrefix="/bfg-watchfaces" />
      </intent-filter>
      <!--
        WITHOUT THIS, onMessageReceived IS NEVER CALLED. A
        WearableListenerService only receives the event types its filter
        DECLARES, and this one declared CHANNEL_EVENT alone — so the class had
        an onMessageReceived override that the system had no reason to invoke.

        Two things were broken by it, and only the second was noticed:

        "Note from your phone" has never worked. NoteSender sends the note on
        MessageClient, which is fire-and-forget, so the phone reported success
        every time and the watch was never told. That is the same shape as the
        transfer bug in DECISIONS.md 2026-08-30 — an API that reports success
        for delivery it cannot see.

        And the catalog request added on 2026-09-19 went nowhere, which is what
        finally surfaced it: the operator updated both apps, opened the picker,
        and the list still did not change.

        The pathPrefix covers both — /bfg-watchfaces/note and
        /bfg-watchfaces/catalog — so nothing here needs to know the paths.
      -->
      <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="com.google.android.gms.wearable.MESSAGE_RECEIVED" />
        <data android:scheme="wear" android:host="*" android:pathPrefix="/bfg-watchfaces" />
      </intent-filter>
    </service>

    <!--
      Hosts the one irreversible permission request, which Android only allows
      from an Activity. No layout: the system dialog is the interface, and the
      explanation already happened on the device before the face was sent.
    -->
    <!--
      No android:noHistory here, and it must not come back. It finishes this
      activity as soon as the permission dialog covers it, so the result
      callback never arrives and the one irreversible answer is silently lost.
      Observed on Wear OS 6, 2026-08-29 — see ActivationRequestActivity.
    -->
    <!--
      The launcher entry. Until 2026-08-29 there was none: the app was a
      listener service and a permission activity, so installed from Play it
      was invisible and a tester had no way to open it or tell whether it
      worked. See WatchActivity.
    -->
    <!--
      THIS APP ANSWERING A COMPLICATION.
      Registered exactly the way ProviderCatalog looks for one — the same intent
      action and SUPPORTED_TYPES metadata it filters on — so the slot picker in
      the phone app offers it with no change to the picker at all.

      UPDATE_PERIOD_SECONDS is 0 on purpose. The value changes when a person
      changes it and never on a timer, so polling would wake the watch to re-read
      a file that is almost always the same. PhoneNoteService.notifyChanged
      pushes instead, at the one moment there is something new.
    -->
    <service
        android:name=".PhoneNoteService"
        android:exported="true"
        android:label="Note from your phone"
        android:permission="com.google.android.wearable.permission.BIND_COMPLICATION_PROVIDER">
      <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.support.wearable.complications.ACTION_COMPLICATION_UPDATE_REQUEST" />
      </intent-filter>
      <meta-data
          android:name="android.support.wearable.complications.SUPPORTED_TYPES"
          android:value="SHORT_TEXT" />
      <meta-data
          android:name="android.support.wearable.complications.UPDATE_PERIOD_SECONDS"
          android:value="0" />
    </service>

    <!--
      THE LAUNCH THEME GOES HERE, NOT ON <application>.

      This is the activity a person starts, so this is the window whose splash
      Wear's branded launch is about. Version 1030 set the splash attributes on
      <application> and nothing on the activity; the launch theme has to be the
      one the starting window resolves. See res/values/themes.xml.
    -->
    <activity
        android:name=".WatchActivity"
        android:exported="true"
        android:label="BFG Watch Faces"
        android:theme="@style/Theme.BfgWatchFaces.Starting"
        android:taskAffinity="">
      <!--
        Opened BY THE PHONE, through RemoteActivityHelper, at the one moment it
        matters: a face has landed and the activation permission has not been
        asked for.

        Without this the wearer had to know, unprompted, to open an app on
        their watch. The permission can only be requested on the watch, the ask
        is a notification, and a fresh install holds no notification permission
        either — so the first face installed, nothing switched, and no prompt
        of any kind could appear. Measured on a Pixel Watch 5, 2026-09-01:
        "notifications are not enabled; the activation ask cannot be shown".

        RemoteActivityHelper requires ACTION_VIEW with BROWSABLE and a data
        URI, which is what this filter is.
      -->
      <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.VIEW" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.DEFAULT" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.BROWSABLE" />
        <data android:scheme="bfgwatchfaces" android:host="setup" />
      </intent-filter>
      <intent-filter>
        <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
        <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
      </intent-filter>
    </activity>

    <activity
        android:name=".ActivationRequestActivity"
        android:exported="false"
        android:theme="@android:style/Theme.Translucent.NoTitleBar"
        android:excludeFromRecents="true" />
  </application>
</manifest>
